{
  "session": [
    {
      "name": "todo",
      "description": "Track a plan for multi-step work.\n\nActions:\n  \"set\":  replace the whole plan (required: todos).\n  \"list\": read the current plan.\n\nWrite the full list every time rather than editing single items. Exactly one item may be in_progress.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "action": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "set",
              "list"
            ]
          },
          "todos": {
            "type": "array",
            "description": "The complete plan, required for \"set\".",
            "items": {
              "type": "object",
              "properties": {
                "content": {
                  "type": "string"
                },
                "status": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "enum": [
                    "pending",
                    "in_progress",
                    "completed"
                  ]
                }
              },
              "required": [
                "content",
                "status"
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "action"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "memory",
      "description": "Remember something across sessions.\n\nTargets:\n  \"memory\": your own notes (environment facts, project conventions, tool quirks).\n  \"user\":   what you know about the person (preferences, habits, how they work).\n\nActions:\n  \"add\":     store a new entry (required: content).\n  \"replace\": swap an entry out (required: match, content).\n  \"remove\":  forget an entry (required: match).\n\n\"match\" is a short fragment that identifies exactly one entry, not the whole text.\nWrites land on disk immediately but only reach your system prompt next session.\nKeep this curated: store what stays true, not what merely happened.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "target": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "memory",
              "user"
            ]
          },
          "action": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "add",
              "replace",
              "remove"
            ]
          },
          "content": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The entry text, for add and replace."
          },
          "match": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "A short unique fragment, for replace and remove."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "target",
          "action"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "cronjob",
      "description": "Schedule the agent to run a prompt on its own: on a clock, once at a moment, on an incoming webhook, or both.\n\nActions: \"create\" (prompt, and schedule, run_at or in_minutes, and/or webhook: true; optional name),\n\"list\", \"update\" (id, and any of name/schedule/run_at/in_minutes/until/prompt/enabled), \"pause\" (id),\n\"resume\" (id), \"remove\" (id), \"run\" (id, to fire it now, only when the user asks: a run\nrepeats what the routine sends and spends credits).\n\nWhen an update changes what a routine does, change its name to match in the same call.\nThe name is what the user sees in the Routines panel and what you read back in \"list\";\none that describes the old job is a routine both of you will misread later.\n\nA routine that already does the job is never made twice: \"create\" hands back the existing\none instead, and nothing fires. Read \"list\" before saying a routine does not exist.\n\nSchedules are five-field cron in local time: \"minute hour day month weekday\".\n  \"0 9 * * *\"    every day at 09:00\n  \"*/15 * * * *\" every fifteen minutes\n  \"0 9 * * 1\"    Mondays at 09:00\nNames like MON are not supported, and neither is a seconds field.\nReach for sensible defaults: pin loose asks to weekdays and waking hours unless the\nroutine is about the user's life rather than their work, and inherit the current minute\nwhen the user names only an hour: asked at 1:32, \"daily at 2\" means \"32 2 * * *\".\n\nFor something that happens once (\"remind me in 10 minutes\", \"alert me at 8:20 tomorrow\"),\ngive in_minutes or run_at instead of a schedule: it fires once at that moment and is then done.\nNever stand in for a one-time, random-time or once-within-a-window job with a frequent\nschedule and a prompt that skips the extra fires: every fire is a full run that can act,\nand a skip left to the prompt can fail. Pick one moment instead.\n\nFor a job that waits for something (\"tell me when the download finishes\", \"alert me\nwhen the price drops below 50\"), give a schedule to check on and until: what it waits\nfor, in a few words. The run that finds it has happened ends the routine, and the app\nretires it, so the prompt need not say when to stop. A routine without until never\nends on its own. until is for routines in this app, not runner: \"hosted\".\n\nA routine with a schedule also fires once the moment it is created, whatever its\nschedule says, so the user sees it work instead of waiting out the first gap. Tell them\nso, and write the prompt so an off-schedule first run still makes sense. When this\nconversation has just done the routine's work itself, pass firstRun: false so it is\nnot done twice.\n\nwebhook: true makes the routine firable by an outside POST; the user copies its URL\nfrom the Routines panel. The request body arrives as data in the fire prompt.\n\nEvery fire runs in a fresh session of its own, listed under Routines, and nothing\nfrom it reaches this conversation: write the prompt to stand alone, and never\npromise to report back here. In a bot's own chat the routine belongs to the bot\nand runs in its voice, with what the bot remembers.\n\nRoutines run on this computer, only while it is on and the app is open, and are\nunlimited on every plan, though each run spends credits like a chat turn. They have\nfull access: they read and write files, run commands and use connectors like this\nconversation.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "action": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "create",
              "list",
              "update",
              "pause",
              "resume",
              "remove",
              "run"
            ]
          },
          "id": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "name": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Short display name for the Routines panel, on create or update."
          },
          "schedule": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Five-field cron expression, for a repeating routine."
          },
          "in_minutes": {
            "type": "number",
            "description": "A one-time routine: fire once, this many minutes from now, then be done."
          },
          "run_at": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "A one-time routine: fire once at this moment (ISO 8601; without an offset, this computer's local time), then be done."
          },
          "until": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "What a routine that waits for something waits for, e.g. \"the model download has finished\". The run that finds it holds ends the routine. update: \"\" clears it."
          },
          "webhook": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "create: also mint a webhook URL that fires this routine."
          },
          "prompt": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "What to ask the agent when it fires."
          },
          "enabled": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "update: enable or pause the job."
          },
          "firstRun": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "create: false skips the fire on creation, for a routine whose first pass this conversation has just done."
          },
          "distinct_from": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "create: the id of an existing routine you have read and this one is a separate job from. Only after create named it."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "action"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "artifact",
      "description": "Build a page people can use: a report, dashboard, one-pager, diagram or small working\ntool. It opens beside the chat and the user can share it by link. A short answer stays\nin the chat. Read the artifact-design skill first.\n\nActions:\n  \"create\":  a new page (title, body). `body` is what goes inside <body>; the page's\n             head, palette and rules are added for you.\n  \"edit\":    small changes (slug, edits). Each `old` must appear exactly once.\n  \"restore\": go back to a saved version (slug, version).\n\nEvery action saves a new version and returns the page's path. Hand that path to\npresent_deliverable to show it.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "action": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "create",
              "edit",
              "restore"
            ]
          },
          "title": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "create: a short real name."
          },
          "body": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "create: the markup inside <body>."
          },
          "slug": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "edit, restore: the page's slug, as create returned it."
          },
          "edits": {
            "type": "array",
            "description": "edit: exact replacements, in order.",
            "items": {
              "type": "object",
              "properties": {
                "old": {
                  "type": "string"
                },
                "new": {
                  "type": "string"
                }
              },
              "required": [
                "old",
                "new"
              ]
            }
          },
          "version": {
            "type": "number",
            "description": "restore: the version."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "action"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "serve",
      "description": "Serve a directory over http and get back a URL, so a web page you wrote can actually\nbe opened. Static files only: html, css, js, images.\n\nUse it the moment you have written a page the user is meant to look at. `bash` cannot\ndo this: it runs a command to completion, so a dev server started there is killed as\nsoon as it reports being ready, and the URL answers nothing.\n\nThen hand the URL to present_deliverable: that is what opens the preview pane. A URL\nin prose is not previewed and not recorded.\n\nActions:\n  \"start\": serve a directory (directory). Serving it again returns the same URL.\n  \"stop\":  stop serving one (directory).\n  \"list\":  what is being served right now.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "action": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "start",
              "stop",
              "list"
            ],
            "description": "What to do."
          },
          "directory": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The directory to serve. A relative path resolves against the workspace directory."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "action"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "present_deliverable",
      "description": "Hand the finished work over. Call this at the end of a turn that produced files,\nlisting what the user asked for, most important first.\n\nThe items become a files card in the chat and are filed as artifacts; in a session\nthe first one also opens in the preview pane. Naming a path in prose does none of\nthat, and a file written with `bash` is not recorded anywhere unless it is declared\nhere. To hand a file to a person on a messaging channel, use send_chat_message with\nattachment_path as well.\n\nList the deliverables, not the workings: the report, not the six scratch files it\nwas assembled from.\n\nCall it again whenever the user asks to see, show, open, or look at something you\nalready made. The pane may have been closed or the app restarted since, and this is\nthe only way to put the file back on screen. Describing it, or rendering its pages,\ninto the chat, does not show it. Presenting the same file twice is cheap and safe.\n\nA URL on this computer is checked first: it must answer, and its page is loaded in a\nbrowser and must not throw. One that fails is not presented, and the result says what\nwent wrong: fix it and present it again. Never tell the user an app is done or fixed\nuntil it has been presented this way. A page `serve` handed out keeps its URL, and is\nserved again when it is opened after a restart.\n\nA file is opened first: an office file (xlsx, docx, pptx, pdf, csv, jsonl) that does not\nopen is not presented, and anything the user would hit (broken formulas, links to another\nworkbook, unfilled blanks) is listed. A translated strings file is compared with its\nsource: give translatedFrom. Fix what it lists before you call the work done.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "items": {
            "type": "array",
            "description": "The deliverables, most important first. The first one is opened in the preview pane.",
            "items": {
              "type": "object",
              "properties": {
                "path": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "description": "Path to the file, or an http(s) URL (a served app). A relative path resolves against the workspace directory. Files must exist and a URL on this computer must work; this reports the ones that do not."
                },
                "translatedFrom": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "description": "For a translated file: the file it was translated from. Every string is compared with its source (missing, untranslated, broken placeholders or numbers, lines shifted onto another id)."
                },
                "label": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "description": "Short human name, e.g. \"Q3 deck (PDF)\". Defaults to the file name."
                }
              },
              "required": [
                "path"
              ]
            }
          },
          "summary": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "One line about what was produced."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "items"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "my_activity",
      "description": "Your own recent activity, across YOUR other conversations: your main\nchat with the user, your auto-reply chats, your routine runs, and the\nchats where other bots asked you questions.\nCall it when the user asks what you have done, or when \"so far\"\nplainly reaches beyond this conversation, and before saying what\nanother bot asked you (or that none did). This chat's context does\nnot follow you between conversations, but your work does.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {}
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "billing_plan",
      "description": "The source of truth for every question about money and limits: the user's Abacus AI plan, credits left and\nwhen they renew, every plan with its price, any first-month discount and what it gives, the limits, and the\nlinks to upgrade or buy credits. Call it for any question, in any wording or language, about what models or\nplans cost, prices, discounts or deals, credits or balance, limits, resets or renewals, upgrading, or what a\nplan includes; whenever they ask for anything to keep working while the app or computer is off or asleep (WhatsApp, Telegram or Discord auto-replies, routines, reminders, monitoring), or to stay online 24/7, or a live, public or shareable link for something they built (hosting, deploying or publishing a site, app or page),\nwhich a plan answers and is never impossible; when they ask to analyse a video (a reel, a Short, a YouTube or TikTok link, a video\nfile); and when they run into a plan limit or run low on credits. Pass upgrade: true whenever they might\nupgrade, so the answer carries their upgrade links.\nNever quote a price, discount, credit amount, limit or reset from memory: call this first, every time, and\nanswer only from what it returns, with every feature line as written (translated, never shortened).",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "upgrade": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "True when they might upgrade: the answer then carries their upgrade links."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "bot_help",
      "description": "Help with this bot's own setup, answered from its live state: its routines (when and where\nthey run, and whether they run with the computer off), its models and provider keys, its\nconnections (connectors and chat apps), its bots and its capabilities. For how do I…, can I…,\nwhere is…, is X connected, does X work when the app is closed, which models can I use, how do\nI connect a chat app or rename a bot. Call it before answering any of these; never answer them\nfrom memory, and never say something is not connected or not possible without it. It names\nthe place in the app for each, which the app shows as a button.\nIt is not for building or publishing apps, websites or pages: that is the user's task, not a\nquestion about this bot. Never call it to check whether you can do a task: when the user asks\nyou to make, build or publish something, use the tools you have for it. Plan prices and\ncredits are billing_plan's. Asked to make an image, video or speech, call paid_feature\nstraight away instead: the plan makes them in Abacus AI Agent on the web.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "topic": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "models",
              "connectors",
              "chat_apps",
              "routines",
              "bots",
              "capabilities",
              "plan"
            ],
            "description": "What the question is about; leave it out for an overview of everything."
          },
          "name": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The one thing asked about, when there is one: a provider (\"gemini\"), a connector (\"gmail\"), a chat app (\"telegram\"), a bot's name, or a capability."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "hand_off_session",
      "description": "Start a separate task in a new chat that runs beside this one, in the same folder with the same model and mode.\nThe task starts at once and the user follows it from a card here and from the sidebar, where it sits under this chat.\nCall it only when it is needed:\n- the user asks for it: to split something off, do it separately, in parallel or in its own chat or session;\n- or a task of its own comes up that does not need this conversation's result and would take long enough to be\n  worth running at the same time (another bug, another feature, another document), and the user agreed or asked\n  for everything to be done.\nNever call it for a step of the current task, for something a few tool calls finish, for a question, or to split\none task into pieces: do those here (delegate_task is for a helper whose result you need back). Never call it\ntwice for the same task. When in doubt, do the work here.\nThe new chat knows nothing of this one: write prompt as a complete brief.\nAfterwards tell the user in one short line, in their language, what you started there, and carry on with the rest here.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "title": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The new chat's name in the sidebar: the task in a few words, in the user's language."
          },
          "prompt": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The new chat's first message, its whole brief, in the user's language: what to do, every detail the user gave, the files or places involved, what is already known or decided here, and what done looks like."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "title",
          "prompt"
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
